  "schema_version": "bookstory.v1",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\"><em>Streaming the Formula 1 Rivalry</em> by Raymond Boyle and Richard Haynes (Peter Lang Verlag, 2024) has accumulated 9 citations within two years of publication, with uptake accelerating in 2026. The work has also been featured in a university webinar, signaling early adoption in teaching contexts. Citation patterns show engagement across leading journals in media studies, sports communication, and leisure studies, with particular traction among scholars examining the intersection of digital platforms and sports media.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">This book offers a timely political-economic analysis of Formula 1 as it navigates digital transformation. Boyle and Haynes examine how streaming platforms and social media have reshaped the sport's relationship with global audiences, sponsors, and media ownership structures—a question of growing urgency as traditional television models compete with on-demand and direct-to-consumer distribution. Published within the Communication, Sport, and Society series at Peter Lang Verlag (ISSN 2576-7232), the work enters an established scholarly conversation in a field marked by sustained publication since 2022.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Citation evidence confirms scholarly uptake, particularly within media and communication research. Nine citing works appeared by early 2026, with five emerging in 2026 alone—a pattern suggesting growing relevance as scholars investigate streaming's structural effects on sports media. Prominent citations appear in <em>Media Culture &amp; Society</em>, <em>Convergence</em>, <em>Leisure Studies</em>, and the <em>International Review for the Sociology of Sport</em>, venues that collectively represent core scholarship on sport-media dynamics. Recent citing works address platform power, symbolic capital in F1 fandom, media technology adoption, and sponsorship strategy, indicating that Boyle and Haynes's framework is being operationalized in multiple research directions.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing landscape spans nine countries, with particular concentration in the United Kingdom (3 citations) and Australia (2 citations), alongside contributions from researchers at Deakin University, Loughborough University, Florida State University, and the University of Bergamo. All citing works originate in academic institutions, confirming the current readership is disciplinary rather than trade or professional. The work is available in open access (green status), which may contribute to accessibility in research environments.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Teaching adoption is documented through a recorded webinar held at UVic Facultat d'Empresa i Comunicació, indicating that the book's analysis has entered pedagogical practice in communication and media studies programs. While comprehensive holdings data and download metrics remain unavailable, WorldCat records show library acquisition, positioning the work as an established reference within academic collections. The early citation trajectory and disciplinary diffusion suggest this book is functioning as an anchor text for scholars working at the convergence of sports, digital media, and platform economics.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
